44. Fundamental CEASE Principle
The fundamental principle of CEASE is:
If you modify CEASE software under the permission granted by this License, you must publish the corresponding source.
This principle applies regardless of:
whether the modification is private;
whether the modification is internal;
whether the modification is commercial;
whether the modification is non-commercial;
whether the software is distributed;
whether the software is hosted;
whether the software is networked;
whether the software is operated locally;
whether the software is used by one person;
whether the software is used by an organization;
whether the organization is a corporation, government, school, nonprofit, or other entity; or
whether the resulting Modified Work is ever provided to another person.
There is no private fork under CEASE.
The fundamental commercial principle is:
CEASE does not prohibit anyone from making money from CEASE software. It prohibits making the underlying CEASE software artificially unavailable as a condition of doing so.
A seller may say:
"Buy our version because we made it easier, better supported, integrated, packaged, customized, maintained, or otherwise more useful."
A seller may not say, in substance:
"Buy this software because you have no practical way to obtain the underlying software."
The purchaser must be told, before purchase, where the underlying CEASE-covered software, its Corresponding Source, and its Upstream Repository can be obtained.
